lazydash patches to CoMotion (public main 1240ba0). Applied by external/bootstrap_comotion.sh.

1. RobotModel copies are safe: the copy constructor/assignment rebuild the
   active-joint table (it pointed into the source's joints_) and give the copy
   a new instanceId(). The Fcl and Vamp backends key per-model caches on
   instanceId() instead of the model address, which a later model can reuse.
2. Allowed inter-robot contacts: RobotModel::setContactId / allowContact /
   contactAllowed. Every inter-robot check of the Spheres and Fcl backends
   skips allowed link pairs (gripper fingers on the grasped object, a
   receiver's fingers on a handed-over object). The Vamp backend refuses
   models with allowed contacts (its kernels cannot skip link pairs).
3. Collision padding: RobotModel::setCollisionPadding, added to sphere radii
   in environment and inter-robot checks of the Spheres backend, never in
   self-collision checks. Fcl and Vamp refuse a nonzero padding.
4. Speed: Fcl tests geometry with the geometry-level fcl::collide plus a
   bounding-sphere pretest (building a CollisionObject per test recomputed the
   mesh AABB from every vertex: ~80% of all instructions); self-collision
   pairs are looked up in a precomputed link mask instead of a string set;
   FK reads joint values by precomputed index instead of a std::map; the
   Spheres and Fcl backends skip robot pairs whose bounding spheres are
   apart.
   Measured (UR5e + Hand-e, one arm, clutter): Spheres 16.6k -> 120-217k
   full checks/s, Fcl meshes 1.4k -> 29-35k full checks/s, same results.
5. Composite path scans (findFirstCompositePathConflict, Spheres and Fcl):
   each robot is posed once per timestep instead of once per pair, and a
   pair whose robots both keep their configuration from the previous
   timestep (valid then) is not re-checked. Fcl mesh geometry (loaded file +
   BVH) is shared by every model using the same mesh, so model copies (new
   instanceId) no longer reload and rebuild it.
   Measured (16 arms, 4 blocks, layer-4 scans): 32.8 s -> 0.4 s, same
   schedules.

